"Decomposes project scope into deliverable-oriented work packages that collectively account for the authorized work.\n\nUse when: Define the complete scope before scheduling activities. Assign and estimate manageable packages without losing their parent deliverables.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns execution sequence rather than scope completeness.\n\nVisual grammar: Parent-child links mean scope decomposition, not precedence. Stable identifiers connect deliverables and work packages to definitions. Peer branches use comparable scope boundaries and granularity.\n\nBuild: State the project outcome and authorized scope boundary. Decompose deliverables until work packages can be estimated and owned. Document package boundaries and reconcile the whole hierarchy against the scope.\n\nCheck: Children collectively cover the parent scope without double counting. Each package has a clear completion definition. Activities and reporting departments do not replace deliverable-based scope.\n\nPitfalls: Reading the left-to-right arrangement as a schedule. Forgetting management, integration or acceptance work required by the scope.\n\nExample: A community hall project decomposes renovation into site preparation, structure, utilities and acceptance packages before estimating the work.\n\nAlternatives: Product breakdown structure, Activity network."
